---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
---

<Site
  title="AirHound"
  description="An open-source WiFi and Bluetooth observation toolkit. Run on desktop, take an ESP32 into the field, or connect an external receiver."
>
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <h1>
      Read the
      <br />
      <em>wireless world.</em>
    </h1>
    <div class="hero-bottom">
      <div>
        <p class="lead">
          WiFi and Bluetooth leave clues. AirHound brings observations from
          desktop radios, pocket-sized detectors and external receivers into a
          shared detection toolkit.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button" href="/start/">
            Choose your setup <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </a>
          <a class="text-link" href="/downloads/">
            Find a release
          </a>
        </div>
        <p class="preview-note">
          Open source · macOS & Linux CLI · ESP32 firmware
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="signal-flow">
        <h2>From radio signal to inspectable evidence</h2>
        <div class="flow-line">
          <span>Observe</span>
          <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <span>Match</span>
          <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <span>Review</span>
        </div>
        <p>
          Keep the observation. Attach the matching rule and its limitations.
          Decide what the evidence actually supports.
        </p>
        <div class="flow-key">
          <span>WiFi frames</span>
          <span>BLE advertisements</span>
          <span>Explicit uncertainty</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <h2>
        Start with the radio
        <br />
        you have.
      </h2>
      <p>
        Different receivers see different things. Pick a setup, check its
        limits, then follow the component guide.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ul class="paths">
      <li>
        <h3>On your desktop</h3>
        <p>
          Scan with the macOS or Linux command line. Export observations as
          NDJSON or WiGLE CSV; add gpsd for receiver-location context.
        </p>
        <a href="/start/#desktop">
          Set up desktop <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Out in the field</h3>
        <p>
          Run dedicated firmware on M5StickC Plus2 or XIAO ESP32-S3. Board and
          runtime validation varies.
        </p>
        <a href="/start/#firmware">
          Choose firmware <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>With an external receiver</h3>
        <p>
          Bring CatSniffer/Sniffle BLE observations into the desktop pipeline,
          or evaluate the experimental ALFA WiFi receiver.
        </p>
        <a href="/hardware/">
          Compare receivers <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section class="section wrap editorial">
    <div>
      <h2>
        A signal is a clue.
        <br />
        Keep its context.
      </h2>
      <div class="statement">
        <p>
          A matching name, vendor prefix or service is evidence. It is not proof
          of a device’s identity or intent.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="body-copy">
      <p>
        AirHound’s shared Rust core attaches rules to observations, with
        explicit review status and limitations. The canonical signature data is
        open to inspection.
      </p>
      <p>
        Broad identifiers can match ordinary devices. Rotating addresses do not
        equal new physical devices. Receiver limits and incomplete payloads
        matter.
      </p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/signatures/">
        Inspect the signature catalog <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section wrap editorial">
    <h2>
      Built in the open.
      <br />
      Improved by evidence.
    </h2>
    <div class="body-copy">
      <p>
        Read the implementation guides, reproduce a hardware test, or contribute
        a well-sourced signature. Experimental paths stay visible, with the
        acceptance work still to do.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button" href="/contribute/">
          Help build AirHound
        </a>
        <a class="text-link" href="/reference/docs/">
          Read the docs
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Site>
